# P2 kernel deficit: what closing it took, and what refused to close

date: 2026-08-07
host: Apple M4 Pro (8 P-core + 4 E-core = 12 logical), macOS 26.5.2, aarch64
rustc: stable, release (lto="fat", codegen-units=1), DEFAULT features
       (bitdepth_8 + bitdepth_16, CHECKED DisjointMut). No -C target-cpu=native.
branch: perf/p2-kernels, off perf/p1-barrier @ 6686b8f (itself off
        perf/shard-tracker @ 34b0505). NOT pushed. PRs #448/#450 untouched.
reference: dav1d 1.5.4 (homebrew), driven with `--framedelay 1` so it runs the
           same threading model we do (tile threading only).
vectors: ~/tmp/rav1d-perf/vec/*.avif — v4k_8tile is 3840x2160, 4 tile cols x
         2 tile rows, 8bpc; v4k_8tile_10b is its 10-bit twin. v1024 / v256 and
         v4k_1tile_10b are used in the correctness sweeps only. dav1d decodes
         the matching .ivf from ~/tmp/recon-yard/vec/, verified elsewhere to be
         the same key frame repeated, so both sides do identical work per frame.

## nice level

NO `nice` ON ANY TIMED RUN. Darwin maps a positive nice value to background QoS
and distorts wall clock by ~40x. `nice -n 19` is for BUILDS only. Every timed
number below is an un-niced run on an idle box behind a >25%-CPU foreign-process
guard that discards and re-runs a contended cell (2 cells discarded and re-run
across the campaign; both were the agent's own polling shell).

Harness gotcha worth keeping: that guard must EXCLUDE the arm binary under test.
macOS `ps` reports a decaying %cpu for a process that has only just exited, so
at t>1 the guard sees the arm it just finished running and discards the cell
forever. `scripts/perf/p2_sweep.sh`'s `busy_count` excludes `bench_*` for
exactly this reason; the first campaign attempt livelocked without it.

## What this was measured against

Starting profile (`benchmarks/p2_kernel_profile_2026-08-07.meta`, taken on
perf/shard-tracker @ 4af62ae): of a 605.8 ms/frame t=1 decode, entropy is at
PARITY with dav1d (212.4 vs 215.4 ms) and the whole 359.5 ms gap is the borrow
tracker (169.5) plus pixel kernels — CDEF 71.7 vs 4.1 (17.3x), itx 67.2 vs 4.7
(14.3x), loop filter 34.2 vs 3.0 (11.3x). Three aarch64 "safe_simd" modules —
`cdef_arm.rs`, `loopfilter_arm.rs`, `looprestoration_arm.rs` — contain ZERO
aarch64 intrinsic calls; their bodies are scalar per-pixel loops.
`ROADMAP_SIMD_PORTING.md` row R3 is the TODO for that.

## Timed campaign

`scripts/perf/p2_sweep.sh`, 6 arms interleaved back to back inside each cell
with the arm order rotating every round, 3 rounds x 3 reps x 4 iters =
MEDIAN OF 9 per cell/arm, 324 rows. Raw:
`benchmarks/p2_kernels_2026-08-07.tsv` (round, vector, threads, arm,
ms/frame, frame md5). Arms are built from the commits, not from a mutated tree.

  vector           t     base    itx8    cdef  lfmask lfbatch    head   base/head
  v4k_8tile        1    547.9   500.0   437.3   432.9   408.7   414.2      1.323x
  v4k_8tile        2    327.2   309.6   278.1   274.0   258.8   260.9      1.254x
  v4k_8tile        4    207.5   204.1   186.6   185.5   174.7   175.8      1.180x
  v4k_8tile        8    200.2   211.6   204.3   203.1   188.5   188.3      1.063x
  v4k_8tile_10b    1    630.9   631.2   631.1   636.3   603.2   601.1      1.050x
  v4k_8tile_10b    8    232.7   233.5   234.0   233.8   212.0   212.1      1.097x

  base    = 6686b8f, the branch point
  itx8    = c4fc055, dispatch the existing 8x8 8bpc NEON itx kernels
  cdef    = 70c1a70, + a real NEON tier for the 8bpc CDEF filter
  lfmask  = 51f76a7, + bounds-check-free CompactTaps
  lfbatch = 2fc646e, + fused loop-filter groups
  head    = 75794ab, + the tile-threading latch (a correctness fix)

Per-change at t=1 8bpc: itx8 -47.9 ms (1.096x), cdef -62.7 ms (1.143x), lfmask
-4.4 ms (1.010x), lfbatch -24.2 ms (1.059x), latch +5.5 ms. The latch is inert
in these runs — every bench process opens ONE decoder, so at t=1 the flag is
never set and at t=8 it is set exactly as before; the +1.3% at 8bpc t=1 is code
layout, and its sign flips at 10bpc t=1 (-2.1 ms) and t=8 (+0.1 ms).

Two things the table says that are worth reading twice:

* itx8 and cdef are BPC8-gated, so the 10bpc column does not move for them at
  all (631.2 / 631.1 / 636.3 vs base 630.9 — noise). All of the 10bpc gain is
  the bit-depth-generic loop-filter work.
* At t=8 8bpc, itx8 is SLOWER than base (211.6 vs 200.2). Making a kernel
  cheaper there just moves the load onto whatever is next in line; the t=8
  bottleneck is not kernel throughput. That is why the composed t=8 gain is
  only 1.063x while t=1 is 1.323x.

## Against dav1d

`scripts/perf/p2_dav1d_wall.sh`, same instrument on both sides: each stream run
at 2 and 20 frames and `total = alpha + beta*frames` fitted so process startup
drops out. 3 rounds, arms rotated, medians. Raw:
`benchmarks/p2_dav1d_2026-08-07.tsv`.

  vector           t     base    head   dav1d   base/dav1d   head/dav1d
  v4k_8tile        1    548.2   413.6   246.7        2.22x        1.68x
  v4k_8tile        8    199.8   188.0    34.6        5.77x        5.43x
  v4k_8tile_10b    1    630.7   600.5   251.4        2.51x        2.39x
  v4k_8tile_10b    8    232.6   213.3    36.1        6.44x        5.91x

The wall fit reproduces the sweep to within 0.3 ms on both our arms, and dav1d
re-measures to within 0.6% of the 246.3 / 34.4 ms on record from the P1
campaign — so the two campaigns are directly comparable.

## Correctness

* BIT-IDENTITY, full corpus: `cargo test --release --no-fail-fast --
  --test-threads=1` run at the branch point AND at head, and every
  `MISMATCH [tier]: <id> expected=<hex> actual=<hex>` triple extracted from
  both. 4,945 MISMATCH lines each side, across 5 CPU tiers (scalar / neon /
  neon-dotprod / neon-i8mm / native) x 989 vectors; 2,845 distinct
  (tier, id, actual) triples each side; **diff = 0 lines**. The failing-test
  set is byte-identical and is exactly the documented 34.
* BIT-IDENTITY, campaign: one frame md5 per (vector, threads) across all 6 arms
  and all thread counts — 324/324 invocations agree
  (a00c11f4... 8bpc, 840d4364... 10bpc).
* Per-kernel oracle: `__simd_test_log` decodes (which run SIMD and scalar on
  every block and diff the pixels) of v4k_8tile / v1024 / v256 log ZERO
  ITX_MISMATCH and ZERO CDEF_MISMATCH after the itx and CDEF changes.
* `-C debug-assertions=on` release builds decode all six local vectors without
  firing the new `CompactTaps` range assert.
* mt_stress passes at threads 1 / 2 / 4 / 8 / 16 (`mt_stress_4k` sweeps them).
* tile_threading_parity 3/3, tile_threading_overlap 3/3, reproduce_overlap 6/1,
  clippy 81 warnings before and 81 after with none in the touched files, and
  zero `unsafe` / `#[allow(unsafe_code)]` added anywhere.

### Overlap detection still fires

* The tracker's own 8 `should_panic` detection tests pass, including
  `concurrent_overlaps_are_caught` and `cross_shard_overlaps_are_all_caught`.
* Live proof on this branch: the detector caught a REAL over-wide borrow — the
  intra-edge bug below — in 8-9 of 24 concurrent runs, with a full backtrace,
  before it was fixed.
* HONEST NEGATIVE: a deliberate over-wide guard planted in `LfBlock::open`
  (one wide `narrow_guard` spanning `(h-1)*stride + w` instead of h per-row
  guards — the shape the codebase documents as unsound under tile threading)
  did NOT fire, either solo or 6-way concurrent (0/18). That says the loop
  filter's rectangle has no concurrent writer on this vector, not that
  detection is broken. A +16-pixel widening of the same guard fired a bounds
  panic instead, which is a different check.

## Soundness finding — pre-existing, fixed here (75794ab)

`TILE_THREADING` is ONE process-global `AtomicBool`, and `rav1d_open` stored
`n_tc > 1` into it unconditionally. Opening a SINGLE-threaded decoder therefore
stored `false` out from under every concurrently live multi-threaded decoder in
the same process. Those decoders' tile workers then took the wide-guard path in
`with_pixel_guard_immut` / `compact_read_per_row`, whose borrow extent is
`(h-1) * stride + w`: a 1x16 intra left-edge column claims 16,321 pixels in
order to read 16. Any concurrent row write inside that span is a real conflict —
a spurious `overlapping DisjointMut` panic that kills the decode in a checked
build, an undetected data race on picture memory in an `unchecked` one.

That is exactly what `tile_threading_overlap`'s `single_threaded_no_panic`
(n_tc = 1) does to `multi_threaded_cdef_lpf_race` (6 threaded decoders) running
beside it in the same binary. The suite passes on an idle box because the store
lands before the threaded decoders start; under load the windows overlap.

  6 concurrent `tile_threading_overlap --ignored` processes, 4 rounds each:
    perf/p1-barrier @ 6686b8f (branch point)   9/24 and 8/24 FAILED
    my c4fc055 (itx only)                      6/24 FAILED
    my HEAD before the latch                   6/24 FAILED
    + the tile-threading latch                 0/24 FAILED

  Pre-fix backtrace: rav1d_prepare_intra_edges (src/ipred_prepare.rs:256)
  -> with_pixel_guard_immut -> narrow_guard (include/dav1d/picture.rs:865)
  -> add_wide -> overlap_panic, `& _[671..16992]` against `&mut _[784..800]`.

Instrument: `scripts/perf/p2_concurrent_overlap.sh`. A single green run of the
committed suite proves nothing about this class — it needs process pressure.

Fix is the minimal correct one: make the setter monotone, never storing `false`.
Per-decoder state would be better but the flag is read from `picture.rs` helpers
that have no decoder context. A purely single-threaded process still never sets
the latch and keeps the fast wide guard.

## What did not land

**16bpc NEON inverse transforms — the kernels exist and are NOT bit-exact.**
`itx_arm_neon_{16x16,32,64,large_rect}` define 16bpc kernels (16x16 all sixteen
types, 32x32 DCT+IDTX, DCT_DCT for 8x32/32x8/16x32/32x16 and the five 64-dim
shapes) and the `asm`-feature FFI wrappers call them, but every arm of the safe
`itxfm_add_dispatch` is gated on BPC8. Wiring them measured, via a
`__simd_test_log` decode: v4k_8tile_10b 5,038 ITX_MISMATCH (all 16x16),
v4k_1tile_10b 5,028 (16x16 + 32x16), v1024_10b 126 (16x16 + 32x32). This is not
rounding drift — nbad = 256, i.e. EVERY pixel of the block, on 3,814 of the
5,038, with max_diff up to 354. eob = 0 DC-only blocks are clean, so the
wiring and unit conversion are right and the transform math is wrong. Fixing
them is the same per-transform work issue #400 did for 8bpc (missing
intermediate clipping, rect2 scaling, shifts). REVERTED; the reason is recorded
in a comment on the dispatch so the next person does not repeat the experiment.

**LF_BATCH_MAX = 8.** Swept: 2 -> 409.4, 4 -> 403.0, 8 -> 406.9 ms/frame
(t=1 8bpc, 3 interleaved pairs each). 8 loses because the larger scratch costs
more zero-init per superblock edge than the extra fusion saves. 4 shipped.

## Where the remaining time goes

Fresh `sample` self-time at t=1 8bpc after the itx and CDEF changes (432 ms/frame
at that point):

  decode_coefs                     45.93%   <- AT PARITY with dav1d, no win here
  borrow tracker (add/remove/wide)  15.9%
  CompInterType guard drop glue      5.05%
  loop filter family                11.13%  <- lfbatch has since taken a bite
  CfL (cfl_pred + cfl_ac_rust)       4.0%
  CDEF (was 11.8%)                   1.8%
  itx  (was 11.1%)                   1.9%
  _platform_memmove                  2.29%

Entropy is ~200 ms of our 414 and ~215 ms of dav1d's 247, so ~200 ms is the
floor at t=1 and the reachable remainder is the tracker (~90 ms), what is left
of the loop filter, and CfL. `looprestoration_arm.rs` is still 1,527 lines of
scalar with zero intrinsics but measured 0.0 ms on this vector (loop restoration
is off in this bitstream), so it is not the next thing to port.
